Ladybird gives ladybird-extension://<id>/ URLs a tuple origin keyed by
the extension’s id. Blink registers chrome-extension as a standard
scheme (url::AddStandardScheme with SCHEME_WITH_HOST in
ChromeContentClient::AddAdditionalSchemes), so its origins are
ordinary tuple origins with the extension id as host. Gecko’s
moz-extension is a standard URL scheme too (URI_STD in
ExtensionProtocolHandler), and each extension’s pages get a content
principal for moz-extension://<uuid>, which BasePrincipal::AddonPolicy()
maps back to the WebExtensionPolicy. WebKit registers webkit-extension
as a custom scheme on the WKWebViewConfiguration and marks it secure
and same-origin-capable through WebExtensionMatchPattern::
registerCustomURLScheme() and the page configuration’s URL scheme
handler, which likewise yields one origin per extension.
